# The two variables the site needs, and the only two. Copy to `.env.local` for
# local development; set both in the Vercel project for a deployment.
#
# Nothing secret belongs here. Vite inlines every `VITE_`-prefixed variable into
# the client bundle, so anything in this file ships to every visitor. That is
# fine for both of these - a contract address is public by construction, and the
# site never holds a key, because every view it calls is read-only and unsigned.

# The deployed ReputationOracle on the network below.
#
# Deliberately empty. It used to carry a studionet address, which stopped being
# a sensible default twice over: it was a redeploy behind, and studionet is no
# longer where this site points. An unset value renders as "unconfigured" rather
# than failing, so an empty value here is a legitimate state and not an error -
# whereas an address from the wrong network reads as an empty registry and looks
# like a bug in the site.
#
# Set it to your own deployment. `npm run settlement` deploys a throwaway
# instance of its own and prints the address, which is the quickest way to get
# one on a network where the payouts actually complete.
VITE_CONTRACT_ADDRESS=

# Which network to read that address from, by the alias the GenLayer CLI uses:
# localnet | studionet | testnet-asimov | testnet-bradbury
#
# Must match the network the address above was deployed to. It is not checked
# against the chain, and a mismatch reads an address that does not exist there,
# which surfaces as an empty registry rather than as a misconfiguration.
# Defaults to testnet-asimov when unset; an unrecognised value throws at startup.
#
# Asimov rather than studionet, and the reason is settlement rather than
# preference: a studio network cannot pay an externally owned account, so every
# release, claim, refund and bond reclaim records the right decision over money
# that never moves. The site says so where it offers those actions. See the
# README's "Settlement" section, and `npm run settlement` for the proof.
VITE_GENLAYER_NETWORK=testnet-asimov
